"""Apply the three-platform, proof-free construction-stage research revision."""
from accounting import ROOT, append, AUDIT, now
import hashlib

path = ROOT / "paper/main.tex"
old = path.read_text()
paragraphs = {
"Many program-verification tasks provide": r"""Many verification tasks supply an independent declarative specification. EqualBench asks whether an agent can prove two fixed implementations observationally equivalent or provide a checked counterexample without a separate functional specification. Each semantic context requires native Lean, native Rocq, and C/CompCert versions. Four implementation groups currently supply twelve concrete contexts, or 36 platform instances, within an inventory of 22 family entries and 54 contexts. Construction uses differential testing and an audited repair loop; programs freeze before proof evaluation. Costs are recorded once per group, shared model usage is counted once, and missing telemetry is disclosed. Native definitions and target types have checked, while Clight export and agent evaluation remain unfinished. Final relational proofs are not required in the current construction stage.""",
"We propose \\textbf{EqualBench}": r"""We propose \textbf{EqualBench} to measure whether AI agents can construct these certificates. Every task context requires native Lean, native Rocq, and C compared through its generated Clight AST and CompCert semantics. Four groups currently supply twelve contexts and 36 platform instances; ports are not independent families. Three earlier simple Rocq probes have recorded proofs. The current construction stage stops at implementations, checked target types, differential evidence and auditable revisions and costs, without requiring final relational proofs. Missing CompCert exports remain explicit incompleteness.""",
"This design draft makes four contributions.": r"""This design draft isolates direct relational verification between fixed implementations without an independently supplied function-level oracle. It defines a two-sided certificate interface and distinguishes native Lean/Rocq comparison from Clight state and memory semantics. Its group-based construction protocol records differential discoveries, repairs, target revisions and costs once per implementation group. The corpus and agent evaluation are incomplete; the intended contribution is a measured benchmark, not this design document alone.""",
"The benchmark certificate goal is": r"""The benchmark goal is the fixed disjunction $\mathit{Eq}(P_A,P_B)\lor\mathit{Cex}(P_A,P_B)$, with polarity hidden from the solver. During construction, labels and test witnesses are provisional; checked reference certificates are recorded separately when available, but are not required at this stage. Truth depends on the frozen programs and boundary, not the curator's expectation. Failure to prove is not evidence of a counterexample.""",
"The native track supplies": r"""Each native version supplies executable functions over an aligned input domain, independently encoded in Lean or Rocq. For total functions, equivalence reduces to equality over all admitted inputs, for example:""",
"For negative instances, the agent must prove": r"""A counterexample must give a valid input and two different successful observations. Some native ports use explicit fuel and return an option; equivalence requires both implementations to return the same successful result on every admitted input. Two exhausted computations cannot pass by returning \texttt{None}. Fuel adequacy remains part of the obligation. Lean supplies checked proposition declarations and proof-free theorem templates; Rocq uses pending declarations closed with \texttt{Abort}, without admitted constants.

Construction and solving have different permissions. A construction model may discover a counterexample and repair or retain an implementation under a new group revision. After freeze, only the proof workspace is editable. Native ports may use structural trees where C uses pointers, so call/control metadata remains platform-specific. Differential cross-language agreement is not a formal translation or refinement theorem.""",
"Agents may add helper definitions, lemmas, and proof terms": r"""Solving agents may add definitions, lemmas and proofs using sound strategies supported by the environment. The evaluator rebuilds protected files and checks the unchanged target with Lean for native Lean, and Rocq for native Rocq and CompCert. Only declared dependencies and axioms are allowed. A checked reference certificate, when obtained, is hidden. Its absence is disclosed rather than replaced by a test-derived proof claim. Final solving success still requires a genuine kernel-accepted certificate.""",
"The original pilot has six families/12 slots": r"""The inventory contains 22 implementation-family entries and 54 semantic contexts: 24 base contexts and 30 from ten complex families. The planned full release targets 60--100 contexts, each with three platform versions. Four groups currently supply twelve concrete contexts and 36 platform instances; remaining contexts are candidates. No new complex target has a final proof. Earlier track-specific IDs are historical catalog labels, not exemptions from the three-platform requirement. Related ports and distinctive shared task code stay in one split group.""",
"Each materialized family fixes": r"""Each concrete group has three intended-equivalent implementations and one near-miss per platform. We select two intended-equivalence contexts and one refutation context, giving eight universal and four existential expected branches per platform; the solver always receives a disjunction. The 36 platform instances refine existing inventory slots without increasing the 54-context count. Labels remain provisional. Appendix~\ref{app:targets} names the obligations.""",
"For construction checks, 660 family inputs": r"""The earlier C-only check executed 660 inputs against four variants, or 2,640 sanitizer-instrumented runs. The revised pipeline additionally runs Lean and extracted Rocq on the same inputs. It compares styles within each platform and each style across platforms, combining boundary cases, seeded random inputs, retained witnesses and explicitly bounded exhaustive subdomains. Native C checks use AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer, not Clight semantic execution. Reports preserve corpus hashes, seeds, source revisions, disagreements and costs. Finite agreement does not certify labels, safety over the full domain or final solving success. No new final relational proof was attempted.""",
"EqualBench can reuse representative implementations": r"""Representative programs can be reused from AlgoVeri, VERINA, Vero and equivalence datasets \citep{zhao2026algoveri,ye2026verina,ye2026vero,wei2025equibench,oza2026cetbench,badihi2021eqbench}. Reuse requires exact source-file/license review, attribution and task-local provenance. Prior labels, tests and specifications aid curation but are not EqualBench certificates. Source-system standalone functional contracts are withheld from the solver. Construction may retain precisely stated unproved problems, with uncertainty disclosed.""",
"Every positive task requires a reference equivalence proof": r"""Construction currently does not require a final target proof. Its gates are source/provenance review, concrete versions on all three platforms, nonempty input boundaries and executable observation code, target-type checking, extensive differential testing, and complete issue/revision/cost records. A constructor may repair an unexpected difference or retain it as a refutation context. Before/after hashes, exact witnesses, affected task IDs and reruns are retained. After freezing a revision, solving agents cannot alter implementations. Construction-ready and reference-ready are separate statuses; pending ASTs or missing accounting evidence block the corresponding readiness claim.

Absence of a differential counterexample only supports a provisional equivalence label. Kernel-checked opposite certificates can later expose wrong labels. Overall solving success is based on accepted certificates regardless of the provisional label; polarity-specific analyses must distinguish certified labels from provisional construction labels.""",
"The immediate pilot goal is": r"""The pilot studies construction quality, then proof feasibility; it is not yet a leaderboard. Three earlier Rocq probes passed recorded \texttt{coqc}/\texttt{coqchk} checks under Coq 8.20.1. Current native definitions and pending target types have checked under Lean 4.34.0 and Rocq 8.20.1; final complex relations remain unproved. No agent proof evaluation has run. A submission must replace design statements with a finalized corpus, explicit label confidence and measured outcomes.""",
"The core uses preallocated arrays": r"""The core uses preallocated arrays, pointer structures, node arenas and bounded work storage. It excludes general allocation, external I/O, floating-point transformations, concurrency and unspecified calls. Source-to-arena adaptations change programs and are disclosed; they do not verify original allocators. Negative C tasks require finite safe witnesses. Native ports use different representations and sometimes explicit fuel, so cross-platform tests are not refinement proofs. Four groups have concrete native/C sources and unproved targets, but Clight export and isolated packaging are pending. Historical resource costs, exact per-group attribution of shared model tokens and hosted inference memory are unavailable; these gaps prevent complete retrospective construction-cost claims.""",
"EqualBench targets proof-producing program comparison": r"""EqualBench targets proof-producing program comparison without an independent declarative functional specification. Each task context requires Lean, Rocq and C/CompCert versions. Construction uses differential discovery and versioned repairs, with costs counted once per implementation group; final target proofs are deferred. Solving still requires a kernel-checked equivalence or counterexample certificate. Completing Clight exports, auditing ports and accounting, scaling the suite and running proof agents remain future work.""",
}
lines = old.splitlines()
for prefix, new in paragraphs.items():
    positions = [i for i, line in enumerate(lines) if line.startswith(prefix)]
    assert len(positions) == 1, (prefix, positions)
    lines[positions[0]] = new
text = "\n".join(lines)+"\n"
text = text.replace(r"\subsection{Native Rocq}", r"\subsection{Native Lean and Rocq}")
text = text.replace("Each family contributes three selected contexts; all proposed relations still require reference certificates.",
    "Each family contributes three contexts with three required platform versions; construction labels remain provisional.")
anchor = r"\subsection{Scoring}"
section = r"""\subsection{Construction Cost and Audit Trail}

The implementation group is the accounting unit. Multiple pair targets and platform ports reference one group record, not duplicated charges. Each local command records UTC boundaries, monotonic elapsed time, user/system CPU time, exit status, arguments, output logs and kernel-reported maximum process RSS, including failures and repairs. Parent envelopes cover orchestration and child commands; nested detail costs are not added again. Maximum process RSS is explicitly the largest single-process high-water mark across waited descendants, not the simultaneous sum of a process tree. Without a dedicated writable cgroup, exact process-tree peak memory is unavailable.

Model tokens come from provider-reported cumulative usage snapshots. Input, cached input, output and reasoning are distinguished; reasoning is already included in output. Shared authoring-session usage is recorded once in a batch and referenced by groups. Where group attribution is unavailable, it is marked unknown rather than evenly divided and called exact. Hosted inference memory and uninstrumented historical work are also unknown. Prefix snapshots state their cutoff; a post-turn snapshot is needed to close the final response's usage. Dollar costs are not inferred without a pinned billing basis.

All discoveries and repairs enter an append-only issue/revision trail. Intentionally retained mutants are distinguished from accidental defects. Compiler/type-check failures remain recorded with costs even after repair. Before/after program hashes and exact differential inputs make changes reviewable; later evidence is bound to the revised source fingerprint. This protocol measures construction effort separately from downstream proof-agent budgets.

"""
assert text.count(anchor) == 1
text = text.replace(anchor, section+anchor)
path.write_text(text)
append(AUDIT / "events.jsonl", {"schema_version": 1, "timestamp_utc": now(), "kind": "manuscript-revision",
    "before_sha256": hashlib.sha256(old.encode()).hexdigest(), "after_sha256": hashlib.sha256(text.encode()).hexdigest(),
    "reason": "Three platforms; mutable audited construction; no final-proof gate; nonduplicated cost accounting"})
print("Updated manuscript motivation, platforms, construction gates, costs and limitations.")
